# Caddy in front of the platform — certificates without a config file per domain.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND THE FRAMEWORK HAS NO CADDY CODE: the platform states one fact through one
# endpoint — "is this host one of ours?" — and every edge consumes that fact in its own way. Caddy is
# the one that can ask at handshake time, so with Caddy a customer domain needs NOTHING here: the
# operator adds it in the admin, the customer points DNS, and the first visitor triggers issuance.
#
# Copy this file, replace the two upstream names and the api address, and run it. Nothing in the
# framework reads it.

{
	# ON-DEMAND TLS. Before Caddy issues for a hostname it has never seen, it GETs this endpoint with
	# `?domain=<sni>` and issues only on a 2xx. 404 means "not ours" and 403 means "ours but
	# suspended" — both refuse the handshake, which is what you want: no certificate is bought for a
	# name this platform does not serve.
	#
	# THE ENDPOINT MUST NOT BE REACHABLE FROM THE INTERNET. It answers only to the platform's internal
	# secret header or to a caller on a private address, and Caddy reaches it over the internal
	# network by container name. Published publicly it becomes a way to enumerate your customers.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://api:3000/api/v1/internal/hosts/permit
	}

	email you@example.com
}

# Every host the platform serves, including customer domains nobody listed here.
#
# `on_demand` is what defers the certificate to first contact. Without it Caddy would need every
# hostname up front, which is the per-domain config step this whole design exists to remove.
https:// {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}

	# The gateway routes by Host: a site's host to the storefront, a workspace's to the admin, an
	# `api.` alias to the api. Caddy does not need to know which is which — that decision already
	# lives in the platform, and duplicating it here is how the two drift apart.
	reverse_proxy gateway:3000 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-Host {host}
	}
}

# --- WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# It does not ask the platform whether a site is PUBLIC. A private site still gets a certificate, on
# purpose: it serves a holding page and its own admins sign in over HTTPS, so refusing TLS would
# replace the holding page with a browser security warning and lock the operator out of the site they
# are building. Who may READ a site is the platform's answer, made per request; this is only about
# whether the name is ours.
#
# --- ORDER MATTERS, BECAUSE OF RATE LIMITS -------------------------------------------------------
#
# Add the domain in the admin FIRST, then have the customer point DNS, and do not probe the host
# until they confirm it resolves. If the platform permits a host whose DNS does not yet point here,
# every hit starts an ACME validation that fails, and Let's Encrypt allows five failures per hostname
# per hour before it starts refusing — a monitoring check on a not-yet-pointed domain will find that
# limit faster than a person can.
